WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.857
Indexing lets us select specific positions from a one-dimensional array.

00:00:04.857 --> 00:00:13.047
Just like Python lists, NumPy uses zero-based indexing, so the first element is at position zero.

00:00:13.047 --> 00:00:16.558
We can also use negative positions to count from the end.

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:20.219
Slicing lets us select a range of values.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:26.009
We can choose the first few values, a middle section, or every second value.

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:33.884
These patterns become even more important when we move to two-dimensional arrays because we will select rows, columns,

00:00:33.884 --> 00:00:36.809
and rectangular sections of a matrix.

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:44.142
For now, make sure you are comfortable reading an index or slice and predicting which values it will return.
